    "result_text": "The build is green. **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 16, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling to store and compare numbers in E.164 form. Changes:\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**\n- Rewrote `normalizeTelephone` to produce E.164: strips non-digit characters (spaces, dashes, brackets); if the input has a leading `+`, its country code is kept as-is; otherwise `+61` is assumed and a single leading `0` is dropped from the national digits. The result must be `+` followed by 8\u201315 digits, else it throws `400`. So `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, and `12` (or blank) is rejected.\n- Duplicate detection now compares the stored E.164 values directly (removed the old \"strip to bare digits\" comparison), so national and international forms of the same number collide (`0412 345 678` vs `+61 412 345 678`).\n- Also normalized telephone on `updateOwner` for consistency with the new stored form.\n\n**`Owner.java` (entity)**\n- Replaced the `@Digits(integer=10)` + `^[0-9]{10}$` telephone validation with an E.164 pattern `^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$`, so persisted entities pass validation.\n\n**`openapi.yml`**\n- Updated the `telephone` field description and example to document the E.164 normalization (`+61412345678`).\n\nAll 16 acceptance tests (Cp01\u2013Cp08) pass, including the new E.164 storage, cross-format duplicate detection, and unformattable-number rejection cases.",
